    "result_text": "The build is green. The test suite only exercises `AcceptanceTest` (which includes the new `coreRejectsDuplicateEmail` test), and it passes.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule that creating an owner whose lower-cased email already belongs to another owner is rejected with **409 Conflict**, following the existing Spring conventions in the codebase (the same pattern used for duplicate telephone/household).\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`DuplicateEmailException.java`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` carrying the offending email, mirroring `DuplicateTelephoneException`.\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 captures the normalized (lower-cased) email, calls a new `requireUniqueEmail(email)` guard before saving, then sets it on the owner.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.requireUniqueEmail`** (new helper) \u2014 skips the check when no email is supplied (`null`), otherwise rejects if any existing owner's email matches case-insensitively (comparing stored values by their lower-cased form, so legacy mixed-case data is still caught).\n\n4. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice.handleDuplicateEmailException`** \u2014 maps the exception to `409 CONFLICT` with `errors: [\"email\"]`, consistent with the other duplicate handlers.\n\nThe check runs before persistence and reuses `findAllOwners()`, so it reflects only owners that existed prior to the create, matching how the telephone/household/city rules behave.",
